  "text_md": "5\n\n4. Embedded Toric Resolution The rational double point singularities are given by one of the following equations:\n\nAn : f(x,y,z) = xy − zn+1, n ∈ N Dn : f(x,y,z) = z2 − x(y2 + xn−2), n ∈ N,n ≥ 4\n\n- E6 : f(x,y,z) = z2 + y3 + x4\n- E7 : f(x,y,z) = x2 + y3 + yz3\n- E8 : f(x,y,z) = z2 + y3 + x5\n\n\nRemark 4.1. Each member of ADE-singularities is Newton non-degenerate.\n\nEmbedded toric resolutions of ADE-singularities were studied in [18, 19]. Here differently we construct regular refinements of Gr¨bner fans of each members of them. This leads us their embedded toric resolutions [1, 2]. Additionally, these resolutions will be minimal which means that:\n\nDefinition 4.2. An embedded toric resolution is called minimal if\n\n- i) The vectors in the regular refinement are irreducible.\n- ii) The resolution graph obtaining from the refinement does not have any −1\n\n\ncurve means the self-intersection of each vertex is different from −1 (this gives us the resolution graph is minimal).\n\nNote that the self-intersection of a vertex is computed as: The number −s ∈ Z≤0 with s.v = mi=1 vi is called the self-intersection of the vertex v in the graph where each vi is adjacent to v in the regular refinement.\n\n4.1. An-singularities. Consider the hypersurface X = V (f) ⊂ C3 where\n\nf(x,y,z) = xy − zn+1, n ∈ N Given v1 ∈ (R≥0)3, let Inv\n\n# (f) = f. The polyhedral cone associated with v1 is Cv\n\n1\n\n# (f) = ⟨(n + 1,0,1),(0,n + 1,1)⟩ Given v2 ∈ (R≥0)3, let Inv\n\n1\n\n# (f) = xy. The polyhedral cone associated with v2 is Cv\n\n2\n\n# (f) = ⟨(0,0,1),(n + 1,0,1),(0,n + 1,1)⟩ Given v3 ∈ (R≥0)3, let Inv\n\n2\n\n(f) = −zn+1. The polyhedral cone associated with v3 is\n\n3\n\n# (f) = ⟨(1,0,0),(0,1,0),(n + 1,0,1),(0,n + 1,1)⟩ The Gr¨bner fan G(X) of X and its Gr¨bner cones can be seen as\n\nCv\n\n3\n\n- Cv1\n- Cv2\n- Cv3\n\n\n(n + 1, 0, 1)\n\n(0, n + 1, 1)\n\n(0, 0, 1)\n\n(0, 0, 1)\n\n(n + 1, 0, 1)\n\n(0, n + 1, 1) (n + 1, 0, 1) (0, n + 1, 1)\n\n(n + 1, 0, 1)\n\n(0, n + 1, 1)\n\n(1, 0, 0) (0, 1, 0)\n\nG(X)\n\n(1, 0, 0) (0, 1, 0)\n\n# Figure 1. The Gr¨bner fan and its Gr¨bner cones for Ansingularities\n\n(0, n + 1, 1)\n\n",
